# Sprint: Checkout Seeing Tool - Development Only Data Integrity Visibility
# Date: 2026-04-11 | Source: Research document + hyper-specific protocol
# Purpose: Development-only seeing tool for checkout flow data integrity
# Constraint: ZERO production interference, simple, robust, 100% trustworthy

# PHASE 0: Pre-Work Lessons Applied
# Keywords: ["data-integrity", "event-logging", "redis", "checkout-flow", "debugging"]
# Critical Lessons Applied:
# - Directness Principle: No mocking core functionality
# - Human-First Verification: Manual verification before tests
# - Build Time Destruction: NO npm run build during development
# - Live Console Debugging: Use console.log for data flow visibility

# PHASE 1: UX Flows First - DEVELOPER EXPERIENCE
# 1. Developer runs checkout flow locally
#   -> System logs ALL events to Redis list
#   -> Developer can query entire flow by idempotencyKey
#   -> Developer sees data integrity violations in real-time

# 2. Developer opens seeing tool UI
#   -> System shows all checkout flows in progress
#   -> Developer clicks flow to see complete event timeline
#   -> System highlights data mutations and integrity checks

# 3. Developer investigates issue
#   -> System shows before/after states for each event
#   -> System shows correlation between events across Redis/Stripe/Sanity
#   -> Developer can replay flow to understand corruption point

# End-State Overview:
# Developers can see entire checkout flow event stream with all data mutations in a single view,
# enabling immediate detection of data integrity issues without affecting production.
# The tool runs only in development and provides 100% trustworthy visibility into
# stock reservations, payment intents, and session state.

# PHASE 2: Architecture Contract

# Event-State-Server Flow:
# Event -> Log to Redis -> Update Integrity Monitor -> Notify Dev UI -> New State

# Three Readable Contracts:

# 1. Events + Payloads
interface CheckoutEvent {
  timestamp: string;
  correlationId: string;
  slice: "basket-address" | "address-submit" | "payment-init" | "payment-submit" | "webhook";
  event: string;
  data: any;
  outcome: "success" | "error";
  error?: any;
}

# 2. Transition Table
# Any checkout action -> Log event -> Check integrity -> Update UI

# 3. Context Shape
interface SeeingToolState {
  activeFlows: Map<string, CheckoutEvent[]>;
  integrityViolations: Array<{
    correlationId: string;
    type: string;
    description: string;
    timestamp: string;
  }>;
}

# Simplicity Guardrail:
# "If it can be done with Redis list + console.log, do it that way"

# PHASE 3: Tiny Scope Contracts (MAX 5)

## Scope Contract 1: Event Stream Logger
### UX Slice
- Developer runs checkout flow -> events logged to Redis -> developer can query by idempotencyKey

### Architecture Slice
- Middleware on checkout actions logs structured events
- Uses existing Redis infrastructure
- No performance impact on production

### Human Verification Checklist (<5 minutes)
- [ ] Run checkout flow with test product
- [ ] Check Redis list contains events
- [ ] Verify correlationId matches idempotencyKey
- [ ] Confirm events are queryable by correlationId

### Minimal Tests
- Test event logging format consistency
- Test Redis list append operation

## Scope Contract 2: Data Integrity Monitor
### UX Slice
- Developer runs flow -> integrity checks run -> violations logged immediately

### Architecture Slice
- Monitors Redis hash consistency
- Validates stock reservation atomicity
- Checks JSON reservation format
- Runs periodic checksum verification

### Human Verification Checklist (<5 minutes)
- [ ] Trigger stock reservation
- [ ] Verify integrity check passes
- [ ] Force corruption (test) -> violation logged
- [ ] Check violation appears in UI

### Minimal Tests
- Test integrity check on valid data
- Test violation detection on corrupted data

## Scope Contract 3: Development UI Interface
### UX Slice
- Developer opens /dev/seeing-tool -> sees active flows -> clicks flow to see timeline

### Architecture Slice
- Simple Next.js page (development only)
- Reads from Redis event list
- Shows events grouped by correlationId
- Highlights integrity violations

### Human Verification Checklist (<5 minutes)
- [ ] Navigate to /dev/seeing-tool
- [ ] Verify page loads only in development
- [ ] Start checkout flow -> appears in UI
- [ ] Click flow -> see event timeline

### Minimal Tests
- Test UI renders events correctly
- Test development-only route protection

## Scope Contract 4: Event Query Interface
### UX Slice
- Developer queries by idempotencyKey -> sees complete flow -> can filter by slice

### Architecture Slice
- API endpoint: GET /dev/seeing-tool/events?idempotencyKey=X
- Optional filter: ?slice=address-submit
- Returns structured event array

### Human Verification Checklist (<5 minutes)
- [ ] Call API with valid idempotencyKey
- [ ] Verify response contains all events
- [ ] Test slice filter returns subset
- [ ] Test invalid key returns empty array

### Minimal Tests
- Test API response format
- Test slice filtering logic

## Scope Contract 5: Integrity Violation Alerts
### UX Slice
- Integrity violation detected -> logged to Redis -> UI shows alert

### Architecture Slice
- Violation logging to separate Redis list
- Real-time updates in UI via polling
- Clear violation descriptions

### Human Verification Checklist (<5 minutes)
- [ ] Trigger known violation
- [ ] Verify violation logged
- [ ] Check UI shows alert
- [ ] Confirm alert includes correlationId

### Minimal Tests
- Test violation logging
- Test UI alert display

# PHASE 4: Continuous Verification

### Per Scope Contract Workflow
1. Implement scope contract
2. Run human verification checklist IMMEDIATELY
3. Run minimal tests (if any)
4. Only then: move to next scope contract

# PHASE 5: Final Human Check

### End-to-End Verification
- [ ] Complete full checkout flow
- [ ] Verify all events logged
- [ ] Check integrity monitor passes
- [ ] Confirm UI shows complete timeline
- [ ] Test query interface returns correct events
- [ ] Verify violations detected and shown

# PHASE 6: Scope Lock Rules

### DO NOT TOUCH:
- Production checkout flow (app/(store)/checkout/*)
- Stripe integration (lib/stripe.ts)
- Sanity client configuration
- Redis production keys
- Any user-facing checkout components

### ONLY MODIFY:
- Development-only routes (/dev/*)
- Event logging middleware
- Integrity monitoring functions
- Development UI components

### ZERO PRODUCTION INTERFERENCE:
- All new code behind dev-only checks
- No performance impact on production
- No production database writes
- No user-facing changes

# PHASE 7: Sprint Lock Criteria

### ALL must be GREEN before completion:
[ ] L1. Event logger captures all checkout events
[ ] L2. Integrity monitor detects violations
[ ] L3. Development UI shows active flows
[ ] L4. Query interface returns correct events
[ ] L5. Violation alerts appear in UI
[ ] L6. Zero production interference verified
[ ] L7. Development-only route protection works
[ ] L8. Redis list operations performant
[ ] L9. Event format consistent across slices
[ ] L10. Manual verification of complete flow visibility

# PHASE 8: Implementation Priority

### Execution Order:
1. Event Stream Logger (foundation)
2. Data Integrity Monitor (core value)
3. Development UI Interface (visibility)
4. Event Query Interface (access)
5. Integrity Violation Alerts (completion)

# PHASE 9: Success Definition

### Sprint Complete When:
- Developer can run checkout flow and see ALL events in single view
- Data integrity violations are detected immediately
- Tool works only in development (zero production impact)
- Implementation is simple and robust (Redis + console.log pattern)
- 100% trustworthy visibility into checkout data integrity
